Description: Fast k-nearest neighbor (K-NN) and principal component
    analysis (PCA) imputation of missing values in high-dimensional
    numeric matrices, such as DNA methylation data. PCA imputation can
    use a locally optimal block preconditioned conjugate gradient (LOBPCG)
    eigensolver, which warm-starts each iteration from the previous
    eigenblock and search direction. Group-wise imputation, for example
    by chromosome, is recommended for Illumina DNA methylation arrays
    such as 450K and EPIC, and for other matrices with groupable columns.
    Sliding-window imputation imputes columns ordered by position. Tuning
    functions choose hyperparameters by masking observed values, imputing
    them, and scoring the result over repeated random masks. The Bayesian
    PCA sampler draws multiple imputations of the missing values. For
    whole-genome methylation data, such as whole-genome bisulfite
    sequencing (WGBS) or Enzymatic Methyl-seq (EM-seq), the sampler is
    recommended because such data are sparsely observed. The K-NN method
    is described in Troyanskaya et al. (2001)
    <doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/17.6.520>. PCA imputation is an optimized
    reimplementation of imputePCA() from the 'missMDA' package, described
    in Josse and Husson (2016) <doi:10.18637/jss.v070.i01>. The sampler
    implements the Bayesian method of MIPCA() in 'missMDA', described in
    Audigier, Husson and Josse (2016) <doi:10.1080/00949655.2015.1104683>.
